      "tool_use_id": "call_00_c1y5MZuQSjfdfLLqh3dx8065",
      "arguments": {
        "subagent_type": "kernel-profile-analyzer",
        "description": "Profile selu_ round 1",
        "prompt": "Profile measured round 1 of the KernelGen workspace at /data/hanle/ascend-optimization/runs/paired-pointwise-20261022/kg-runs/selu_-paired-pointwise-20261022-paired-hgkda/stages/optimize/work (round_num = 1).\n\nContext: the op is in-place SELU (selu_) implemented as a Triton elementwise kernel with a cached raw Ascend launcher path. Baseline geo_mean speedup 0.730. Observations from the authoritative eval:\n- Launch-bound small workloads (selu_-timing-0/1/2/4/5/6/8/9/10, numel 1024..1048576): candidate ~16.0-17.5 us vs reference ~11.8-14.2 us (speedup 0.72-0.84).\n- Large 4096x4096 workloads: selu_-timing-3 (fp16) 81.9us vs 56.9us; selu_-timing-7 (bf16) 83.2us vs 59.1us; selu_-timing-11 (fp32) 82.7us vs 49.4us (speedup 0.60-0.71).\n\nI need to decide the single highest-value next experiment. Please collect backend-native (msprof) evidence and answer specifically:\n1. For the 4096x4096 workloads (timing-3/7/11): what is the pure device kernel duration and what fraction of the ~82us wall time is host launch overhead? Is the kernel bandwidth-bound or instruction/compute-bound (e.g. is the exp/select path the limiter)?\n2. Which hardware pipeline is saturated on the big shapes (MTE2/MTE3/VEC/CUBE ratios, UB usage, sync stalls), and what is the achieved bandwidth?\n3. For the small launch-bound workloads, is there measurable device-side kernel time at all, or is it entirely host launch latency?\n4. Recommend ONE concrete, highest-expected-value code change for the Triton kernel (e.g. tile-size change, native low-precision compute path, cheaper exp formulation, more programs per core) with the mechanism and the workloads it should improve.\n\nUse the kernel-profile MCP tools for round_num=1; the round snapshot is immutable and available. Return a concise analysis plus one actionable experiment."
      }
    }
  ],
